.title = "TypeScript",
.description = "Use one TypeScript session API across Deno, Bun, and browser WebAssembly.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

The TypeScript binding supports three runtimes from one source tree.
`@glowcap/runtime`
([`mod.ts`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/ts/runtime/mod.ts))
gives Deno and Bun the `Session` and `Host` API over the glowcap C ABI. That API
does not depend on any transport. `Runtime.open` installs the transport
providers and connectors once, and `runtime.connect` then uses the one that
matches the locator.

- Transport packages such as `@glowcap/iroh` build their own locators and own
  their transport configuration.
- `@glowcap/onion`, `@glowcap/relay`, and `@glowcap/tcp` provide the other
  native providers and connectors shown below.
- `@glowcap/inproc` is a lower-level package that moves raw frames. It is not
  part of the managed Session API yet, because it has no provider to install
  into a Runtime.

The same source also compiles to WebAssembly as `@glowcap/web`, so the same
`Session` runs in the browser, dialing rather than listening.
[In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')) covers how a page reaches its
peers.

Pin a checkout, build the archives, and
run from `bindings/ts` or point an import map at `bindings/ts/runtime/mod.ts`.
Set `GLOWCAP_LIB_DIR` only if the archives are outside `zig-out/lib`. Deno
needs `--allow-ffi --allow-read --allow-net`, plus `--allow-env` when the
library path comes from the environment.

# The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, send,
handle the reply) reads like this in TypeScript. The inbound loop delivers the
partner's replies and dispatches its calls to the actors you publish. `fetch`
and `send` start it on demand, so a caller does not need a separate `serve`
task. Use `serve` when another task needs to wait for session shutdown.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:ts GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { locator, provider, Reachability } from "@glowcap/iroh";
>
> // Discovery resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> const nodeId = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> const partner = locator(nodeId);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	providers: [provider({ reachability: Reachability.Discovery })],
> });
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```ts
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { connector, locator, type SignalingAuthority } from "@glowcap/webrtc";
>
> declare const signaling: SignalingAuthority;
> const peerKey = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the peer>";
> const partner = locator(peerKey);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	connectors: [connector(signaling)],
> });
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```ts
> import { noise, provider } from "@glowcap/tcp";
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
>
> // Same TCP sockets as keyed dialing; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream.
> const peerKey = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> const partner = noise.locator(peerKey, "127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({ providers: [provider()] });
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```ts
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { noise, provider } from "@glowcap/relay";
>
> // Noise_XK seals the session end to end; the relay forwards ciphertext.
> const peerKey = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> const relayUrl = "wss://relay.example/relay";
> const partner = noise.locator(peerKey, relayUrl);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	providers: [provider({ relayUrl })],
> });
> ```
> **onion**
> ```ts
> import { locator, provider } from "@glowcap/onion";
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
>
> // glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> const partner = locator("<56-character onion service id>");
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	providers: [provider({ socks: "/run/tor/socks" })],
> });
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```ts
> import { keyed, provider } from "@glowcap/tcp";
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
>
> // Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Partners dial the public key; the host:port is a hint.
> const peerKey = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> const partner = keyed.locator(peerKey, "127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({ providers: [provider()] });
> ```
> **keyed-relay**
> ```ts
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { keyed, provider } from "@glowcap/relay";
>
> // The key names the peer; the hint names the relay that can reach it.
> const peerKey = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> const relayUrl = "wss://relay.example/relay";
> const partner = keyed.locator(peerKey, relayUrl);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	providers: [provider({ relayUrl })],
> });
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { locator, provider } from "@glowcap/tcp";
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
>
> // Plain TCP uses a cleartext host and port.
> const partner = locator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({ providers: [provider()] });
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```ts
await using session = await runtime.connect(partner);
const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
console.log(reply);
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:ts -->
```

The CapTP verbs stay the same across tabs. What changes is the locator helper,
the listen/seed posture, and whether CapTP rides cleartext or Noise. The iroh
tab needs `@glowcap/iroh` and a discovery-reachable node id.

The `serve` promise resolves when the session closes cleanly and rejects on a
transport or protocol error. When the loop ends, every `send` still awaiting a
reply breaks too. Failures surface on the calls you await; the bare `catch`
only keeps the loop's own rejection from going unhandled.

# Hosting the other half

The hosting side reads the same way. `listen` binds a port and returns a
`Server` you iterate for each accepted `Session`, `publish` makes an actor
fetchable by name, and `serve` dispatches the partner's calls to it. Keyed and
Noise listeners take a 32-byte seed so the advertised designator survives a
restart; iroh binds a node id instead of a host and port.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:listen:ts GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import type { Argument } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { type IrohConfig, listen, Reachability } from "@glowcap/iroh";
>
> // This listener is named by its node id. Partners can dial it from anywhere.
> const config: IrohConfig = { reachability: Reachability.Discovery };
> await using server = await listen(config);
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```ts
> import type { Argument } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { noise } from "@glowcap/tcp";
>
> // Same bind as keyed-tcp; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> const seed = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));
> await using server = await noise.listen("127.0.0.1", 9099, { seed });
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```ts
> import type { Argument } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { noise } from "@glowcap/relay";
>
> // Register this key at the relay; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> const seed = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));
> await using server = await noise.listen({
> 	relayUrl: "wss://relay.example/relay",
> 	registerUrl: "wss://relay.example/register",
> 	seed,
> });
> ```
> **onion**
> ```ts
> import type { Argument } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { listen } from "@glowcap/onion";
>
> // glowcap starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> await using server = await listen({
> 	socks: "/run/tor/socks",
> 	control: "/run/tor/control",
> });
> const _serviceKey = server.serviceKey; // persist securely to restore this service
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```ts
> import type { Argument } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { keyed } from "@glowcap/tcp";
>
> // Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Reuse a 32-byte seed to keep the same key.
> const seed = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));
> await using server = await keyed.listen("127.0.0.1", 9099, { seed });
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import type { Argument } from "@glowcap/runtime";
> import { listen } from "@glowcap/tcp";
>
> // Plain TCP binds the host and port you choose.
> await using server = await listen("127.0.0.1", 9099);
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```ts
console.log(server.uri); // hand this locator to partners

function greeter(verb: string, args: Argument[]): string {
	if (verb !== "greet") throw new Error("no such method");
	if (args.length !== 1 || typeof args[0] !== "string") {
		throw new Error("bad invocation");
	}
	return `Hello, ${args[0]}`;
}

server.host.publishGiftable("greeter", greeter);
for await (const accepted of server) {
	await using session = accepted;
	await session.serve();
}
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:listen:ts -->
```

Over iroh there is no host and port to bind. The server reports its node-id
locator through `server.uri`.

# Async

Sessions return native promises, and a session is disposable: connect with
`await using`, and the session closes when the block ends, in place of an
explicit `session.close()`. For how one call can pipeline on another before it resolves,
see [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')).

# Actors

An actor is a callback, `(verb, args) => AppValue`, that you register with
`session.spawn` or `session.publish`; it returns any value you can send, and the
partner receives that as the reply. A throw becomes the caller's broken promise.
To hand one actor across an introduction so a third peer can reach it directly,
publish it on a `Host` with `publishGiftable`. You hold a `Host` as `server.host`
from `listen`, or mint one with `new Host()`. The `Host` is the multi-session
product in [modules]($link.page('modules')), and
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains the three-party handoff a giftable
rides on.

The complete API lives in the
[TypeScript binding README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/ts/README.md).
The same source runs under Deno and Bun. Deno hosts the native driver loop;
Bun's synchronous FFI cannot, so Bun stays on the byte-fed Host path for
identity and locator checks rather than hosted dial/listen. The wasm build is
[In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')).
